<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander II of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Alexander II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> from 1855 to 1881, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Liberator. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-reforms-russia">Great Reforms</a> included the emancipation of the serfs in 1861, together with changes to local government, the courts and the army. He was assassinated by revolutionaries in 1881.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia.jpg" alt="Alexander II of Russia" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to W. &amp; D. Downey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zar_Alexander_II_(cropped)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Empire's social structure was defined above all by serfdom, which bound roughly a third of the total population, and a far higher proportion of the rural workforce, to the land and to the legal authority of noble landowners until Tsar <b>Alexander II</b>'s Emancipation Edict of 19 February 1861.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On 26 August 1856, on the accession of <b>Alexander II</b>, the Decembrists were amnestied and their rights restored, though many chose to stay in Siberia.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Three emperors ruled in these decades: Alexander I (1801–1825), who drove Napoleon’s army out of Russia in 1812; his brother Nicholas I (1825–1855), who began his reign by crushing the Decembrist revolt of noble officers; and Nicholas’s son <b>Alexander II</b> (1855–1881), who freed the serfs.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Hildebrand of Sovana was elected pope as Gregory VII, succeeding Pope <b>Alexander II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-22">22 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At York, Henry III of England and <b>Alexander II</b> of Scotland agreed that Northumberland, Cumberland and Westmorland were subject to English sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-25">25 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alexander II</b> convened the Diet regularly and allowed Finnish to become an official language.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-finland">Grand Duchy of Finland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Small Nicholas Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Winter Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1858" aria-label="Small Nicholas Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander II of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1858">What was here in 1858? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
